WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
The first medical emergency we'll discuss is heart attacks and the difference between a heart attack and cardiac arrest.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
In a heart attack, blood flow to part of the heart becomes blocked, which causes damage to the heart muscle.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
The person is usually still conscious and talking, though they are likely experiencing some of the symptoms shown here.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
It is also vital to understand that a heart attack doesn't look the same for everyone.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.000
While we often think of classic chest pain, women frequently experience much more subtle or atypical signs.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:39.000
This can include things like unusual fatigue, sudden nausea, lightheadedness, or even pain that is concentrated in the jaw, neck, or back, rather than the chest.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Because these symptoms can feel less dramatic, women are sometimes less likely to seek help quickly.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
If you suspect something is wrong based on these signs, don't wait for classic chest pain to appear.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Act on that suspicion and call 911.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
On the other hand, cardiac arrest is when the heart suddenly stops pumping altogether.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
The person instantly becomes unresponsive and stops breathing normally.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.000
That is your cue to start CPR and get an AED immediately because it is an immediate life-threatening emergency.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Now that we know what to look for, let's talk about what is actually happening inside the body during a heart attack and, most importantly, exactly how you should respond.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Inside the chest, a clot is physically blocking the blood flow, which means that specific part of the heart isn't getting any oxygen.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000
If that blockage lasts too long, the heart muscle actually begins to die, which is why we treat this as a race against the clock.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:44.000
The moment you suspect a heart attack, your very first move is to call 911 and have someone grab an AED to have on standby.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:52.000
While you wait for the professionals, help the person sit down on the ground to reduce the workload on their heart and do your best to keep them calm and comfortable.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
If the person is alert and it is appropriate for them, you can have them chew and swallow one adult aspirin.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Keep in mind that a heart attack can often lead directly to cardiac arrest if the heart damage causes the heart to stop pumping.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.000
This is why you must stay right there with them.

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.000
If they ever become unresponsive or stop breathing normally, you need to be ready to transition immediately into CPR and turn on that AED.

